Implementing memory pooling in Unreal Engine can help manage memory more efficiently, especially when dealing with frequently allocated and deallocated objects like road splines. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to implement memory pooling for road splines in Unreal Engine.

### Step 1: Define a Memory Pool Class

First, create a custom memory pool class that will manage the allocation and deallocation of road spline data.

```cpp
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "UObject/ObjectMacros.h"
#include "Engine/EngineTypes.h"
#include "MemoryPool.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class YOURPROJECT_API UMemoryPool : public UObject
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    UMemoryPool(const FObjectInitializer& ObjectInitializer);

    void* Allocate(size_t Size);
    void Deallocate(void* Pointer);

private:
    TArray<void*> FreeList;
};
```

### Step 2: Implement Memory Pool Methods

Next, implement the methods for allocating and deallocating memory from the pool.

```cpp
UMemoryPool::UMemoryPool(const FObjectInitializer& ObjectInitializer)
    : Super(ObjectInitializer)
{
}

void* UMemoryPool::Allocate(size_t Size)
{
    if (!FreeList.IsEmpty())
    {
        void* Pointer = FreeList.Pop();
        return Pointer;
    }
    else
    {
        return FMemory::Malloc(Size);
    }
}

void UMemoryPool::Deallocate(void* Pointer)
{
    FreeList.Add(Pointer);
}
```

