Ensuring that a memory pool is thread-safe in a multithreaded application is crucial to prevent race conditions and data corruption. Here are the steps to make your memory pool thread-safe in Unreal Engine:

### Step 1: Use Mutexes for Synchronization

Mutexes (mutual exclusion locks) can be used to synchronize access to shared resources. Unreal Engine provides `FCriticalSection` for this purpose.

### Step 2: Modify the Memory Pool Class

Update the `UMemoryPool` class to include a `FCriticalSection` for synchronization.

```cpp
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "UObject/ObjectMacros.h"
#include "Engine/EngineTypes.h"
#include "MemoryPool.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class YOURPROJECT_API UMemoryPool : public UObject
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    UMemoryPool(const FObjectInitializer& ObjectInitializer);

    void* Allocate(size_t Size);
    void Deallocate(void* Pointer);

private:
    TArray<void*> FreeList;
    FCriticalSection Lock;
};
```

### Step 3: Implement Thread-Safe Methods

Modify the `Allocate` and `Deallocate` methods to use the mutex for synchronization.

```cpp
UMemoryPool::UMemoryPool(const FObjectInitializer& ObjectInitializer)
    : Super(ObjectInitializer)
{
}

void* UMemoryPool::Allocate(size_t Size)
{
    Lock.Lock();

    if (!FreeList.IsEmpty())
    {
        void* Pointer = FreeList.Pop();
        Lock.Unlock();
        return Pointer;
    }
    else
    {
        Lock.Unlock();
        return FMemory::Malloc(Size);
    }
}

void UMemoryPool::Deallocate(void* Pointer)
{
    Lock.Lock();
    FreeList.Add(Pointer);
    Lock.Unlock();
}
```

